  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe near term for today features the coastal low shifting north\nup the coast with a continued trough edging into the Upper Oh\nValley area creating an environment for additional scattered \nshowers across the area. Instability is very little if at all\nwith MU CAPE values in Ohio around 100 - 200 J/Kg. This means\nshowers will be ending by this evening as the last of the\nforcing associated with the trough finally shifts to the east. \n\nHigh pressure will filter into the area by Thursday nosing south\ninto the area from the height center over the northeastern \nCONUS. Temperatures are expected to be a good 5 to 7 degrees \ncooler than normal for Thursday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe below normal temperatures are expected to continue through\nthe upcoming weekend. The high center over the Upper Great Lakes\nwill filter in cooler air into the region. Some locations will\nsee temperatures approach the 30s. The main location in the\nforecast area would be Canaan Valley in eastern Tucker where\nthere is a 90% chance of temperatures below 40 degrees Thursday\nnight. Thus, some frost has been put in the grids over the\nhigher terrain. Temperatures look to be below normal through the\nweekend until Tuesday next week where a warm up is expected. \n\nThe other change is the potential for precip on Saturday from a\ncoastal low. Trends in the ensembles have the low off the coast\nas precip over western PA or any of the PBZ CWA will avoid the\npotential for precip. It should be noted that not even CTP's\narea will see any pops.",
